the current low oil price is a boon for  India the country imported 190 million  tons of crude oil in 2014 and while  other emerging economies were suffering  from lower oil revenues India was saving  money the equivalent of 54 billion euros  according to experts the Indian  government wants to use those savings to  plug holes in its budget deficit and to  further boost economic growth India is  already one of the world's  fastest-growing economies since 2014  official figures have put GDP growth at  more than seven percent this year the  country is expecting growth of seven and  a half percent putting India ahead of  China state revenues are badly needed to  expand roads and railways and to improve  the country's health care and education  systems at the same time bureaucracy  needs to be streamlined and public  services made more efficient many  investors are impressed by India's  strong economic performance in recent  months they've gone on a shopping spree  snatching up shares on indian stock  markets investors have noticed that  strong growth figures have not yet  influenced the country stock prices  since april of last year India's msci  index has fallen by fifteen percent  investors hope stock prices will now  start rising and if global oil prices  remain low India's economy is likely to  further benefit if that is the Indian  government is successful with its  ongoing economic reforms
 

